// Regression test: a `when` exit branch must observe the post-transition
// hotkey grab state for a binding created inside the when body.
//
// what: `pHk = p => ...` inside `when title_ ~ "mpv" {}` registers as a
//   conditional hotkey whose grab follows the when condition. When the
//   when deactivates, grab/enabled must already be false BY THE TIME the
//   sibling `when !(...)` exit branch runs.
// why: the app-side var-change handler (Havel.cpp setOnVarChangedSync, and
//   the same site in HavelEngine) ran when-branch bodies BEFORE the
//   conditional-hotkey re-eval, so the exit branch observed
//   enabled=true grab=true and the flip landed one tick later. Reproduced
//   2026-10-30: exit-branch read enabled=true grab=true, +100ms read
//   enabled=false grab=false. Fix: grab re-eval first, bodies last.
// expected: the exit branch sees enabled=false grab=false; PASS.
// run: ./build-debug/havel scripts/tests/test_when_exit_observes_ungrab.hv
//   (manual-run; registers the `p` hotkey - the grab is brief and released
//   when the when deactivates; no input injection)
sawExit = false
exitOk = true
title_ = ""
pHk = nil
when title_ ~ "mpv" {
  pHk = p => print("P pressed in mpv")
}
when !(title_ ~ "mpv") {
  if pHk != nil {
    sawExit = true
    if pHk.enabled || pHk.grab {
      exitOk = false
      print("FAIL: exit branch sees enabled=${pHk.enabled} grab=${pHk.grab} (bodies ran before the grab re-eval)")
    }
  }
}
title_ = "mpv"
sleep(500)
if pHk == nil {
  print("FAIL: when body did not create the binding")
  exit(1)
}
if !pHk.enabled || !pHk.grab {
  print("FAIL: binding not grabbing inside mpv: enabled=${pHk.enabled} grab=${pHk.grab}")
  exit(1)
}
title_ = "zzz"
sleep(200)
if !sawExit {
  print("FAIL: exit branch never ran")
  exit(1)
}
if !exitOk {
  exit(1)
}
print("PASS: when exit branch observes enabled=false grab=false")
exit(0)
